<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Buchenwald concentration camp</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1937–1945</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Buchenwald was a Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camp</a> on the Ettersberg hill near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, opened in 1937. About 280,000 people were imprisoned there and in its subcamps, and some 56,000 died from hunger, disease, medical experiments and executions. It was liberated by American troops in April 1945, and the Soviets then used it as a special camp until 1950.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/buchenwald-concentration-camp.jpg" alt="Buchenwald concentration camp" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buchenwald_Prisoners_Undressing_80135.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
